{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-339953" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "From **left to right**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x-5}{{x}^{2}-4x-21}$ are\n\n$x=$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339953" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x-5}{{x}^{2}-4x-21} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x-5}{(x-7)(x+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-7)(x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3,\\,x=7\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the vertical asymptotes are $x=-3$ and $x=7,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/56b3d93afca64c37cd48013702f2317f41935e85d5b795c34f31a76927852d44.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339953" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bdf8a028228fc3190b34be40ccd7f60ee5b218cff9bc8e7d00f4b84aaf55fb39"} {"question" "q-339953" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339953" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339953" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339953" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "From **left to right**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}+5x-14}$ are\n\n$x=$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}+5x-14} \\\\ y & =\\frac{8}{(x-2)(x+7)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x+7)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-7,\\,x=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the vertical asymptotes are $x=-7$ and $x=2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/069b968e405a1302f67a085da7aff92979c95d4fca423aad3885ca6267d8cc34.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bdf8a028228fc3190b34be40ccd7f60ee5b218cff9bc8e7d00f4b84aaf55fb39"} {"question" "q-339951" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339951" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-7"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339951" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339951" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "From **left to right**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9-{x}^{2}}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} \n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9-{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{(3-x)(3+x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(3-x)(3+x)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3,3\n$$\n\nConsequently, both $x=-3$ and $x=3$ are vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x),$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/ead2334ba9281edbe8545c4d18b8e64bb72e28915d98674fe68364dfdc4e0a4e.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bdf8a028228fc3190b34be40ccd7f60ee5b218cff9bc8e7d00f4b84aaf55fb39"} {"question" "q-339947" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339947" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339947" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339947" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-47854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)=\\frac{5}{x-\\sqrt{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-47854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "There are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-\\sqrt{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is the only vertical asymptote of $y=f(x),$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/09c19a6049d77a2b57c093ca714627a453c99bf52af27ae656ad1f4e779d1866.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-47854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bdf8a028228fc3190b34be40ccd7f60ee5b218cff9bc8e7d00f4b84aaf55fb39"} {"question" "q-47854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-47854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-2"] ["math" "x=2"] ["math" "x=5"] ["math" "x=\\sqrt{2}"] ["text" "There are no vertical asymptotes."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-47854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-47853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-5x-6}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-47853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-5x-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x-6)(x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-6)(x+1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1,6.\n$$\n\nConsequently, both $x=6$ and $x=-1$ are vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x),$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/b3d4116e1157d2f9b5414a015965b371982663518d04631810f19d8be4a7de93.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-47853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bdf8a028228fc3190b34be40ccd7f60ee5b218cff9bc8e7d00f4b84aaf55fb39"} {"question" "q-47853" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-47853" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-1$ and $x=6$"] ["text" "$x=-3$ and $x=2$"] ["text" "$x=1$ and $x=-6$"] ["text" "$x=3$ and $x=-2$"] ["text" "There are no vertical asymptotes."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-47853" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-1$ and $x=6$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339971" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+8x}{{x}^{2}-4x}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339971" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+8x}{{x}^{2}-4x} & =\\frac{2x(x+4)}{x(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{x}(x+4)}{\\cancel{x}(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+4)}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=4\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339971" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bdf8a028228fc3190b34be40ccd7f60ee5b218cff9bc8e7d00f4b84aaf55fb39"} {"question" "q-339971" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339971" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-37066" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x-6}{{x}^{2}-7x+12}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-37066" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-6}{{x}^{2}-7x+12} & =\\frac{2x-6}{{x}^{2}-4x-3x+12} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-3)}{x(x-4)-3(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-3)}{(x-4)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{(x-3)}}{(x-4)\\cancel{(x-3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=4.\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-37066" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bdf8a028228fc3190b34be40ccd7f60ee5b218cff9bc8e7d00f4b84aaf55fb39"} {"question" "q-37066" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-37066" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=2,x=3"] ["math" "x=3"] ["math" "x=4"] ["math" "x=4,x=3"] ["text" "No vertical asymptotes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-37066" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339976" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-8}{{x}^{2}+7x+10}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339976" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-8}{{x}^{2}+7x+10} & =\\frac{2({x}^{2}-4)}{(x+5)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{2}^{2})}{(x+5)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-2)(x+2)}{(x+5)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-2)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{(x+5)\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-2)}{x+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx+5=0\\,⟹\\,x=-5\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=-5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339976" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bdf8a028228fc3190b34be40ccd7f60ee5b218cff9bc8e7d00f4b84aaf55fb39"} {"question" "q-339976" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339976" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339960" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{4x-8}{2{x}^{2}+2x-12}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339960" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4x-8}{2{x}^{2}+2x-12} & =\\frac{4(x-2)}{2(x+3)(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cancel{(x-2)}}{2(x+3)\\cancel{(x-2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx+3=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339960" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bdf8a028228fc3190b34be40ccd7f60ee5b218cff9bc8e7d00f4b84aaf55fb39"} {"question" "q-339960" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339960" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-37069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{12x}{{x}^{2}-4x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-37069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12x}{{x}^{2}-4x} & =\\frac{12x}{x(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{x-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=4.\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-37069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bdf8a028228fc3190b34be40ccd7f60ee5b218cff9bc8e7d00f4b84aaf55fb39"} {"question" "q-37069" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-37069" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=0"] ["math" "x=12"] ["math" "x=4"] ["text" "$x=0$ and $x=4$"] ["text" "No vertical asymptotes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-37069" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-68167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{3x-2{x}^{2}}{18-12x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-68167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x-2{x}^{2}}{18-12x} & =\\frac{x(3-2x)}{6(3-2x)}=\\frac{x}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/04c9f0c0e0f8ee16e9eee949d23a816b9ad877ab205d3231bb183a486da97544.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-68167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bdf8a028228fc3190b34be40ccd7f60ee5b218cff9bc8e7d00f4b84aaf55fb39"} {"question" "q-68167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-68167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-6"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{3}{2}"] ["text" "No vertical asymptotes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-68167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No vertical asymptotes"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-68151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}{6x+3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-68151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}{6x+3} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}(2x+1)}{3(2x+1)}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/7ca0a626369d11d7d3cd8e2d99cbfb79eb37c534b86a1df3f43aaeff5fb932e3.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-68151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bdf8a028228fc3190b34be40ccd7f60ee5b218cff9bc8e7d00f4b84aaf55fb39"} {"question" "q-68151" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-68151" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{1}{3}"] ["text" "No vertical asymptotes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-68151" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No vertical asymptotes"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-107068" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{(x+4)({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-5x)}{{x}^{2}+4x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-107068" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-5x)}{{x}^{2}+4x} & =\\frac{x(x+4)({x}^{2}+3x-5)}{x(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x(x+4)}({x}^{2}+3x-5)}{\\cancel{x(x+4)}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+3x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=-4$ and $x=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/a10cef51ad5955f6065fbf4deaccbab1cda1f576db96310236ea976bb8d8eb8b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-107068" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bdf8a028228fc3190b34be40ccd7f60ee5b218cff9bc8e7d00f4b84aaf55fb39"} {"question" "q-107068" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-107068" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-4"] ["math" "x=-4,0"] ["math" "x=0"] ["math" "x=0,2"] ["text" "No vertical asymptotes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-107068" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No vertical asymptotes"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-4631" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{(x-1)({x}^{3}+1)}{{x}^{2}-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-4631" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)({x}^{3}+1)}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)({x}^{2}-x+1)}{(x-1)(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}({x}^{2}-x+1)}{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=\\pm 1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/c76cce145059193c5c5348f2de347ac470f35e8b5392e196ac8b9766382bf2b8.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-4631" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bdf8a028228fc3190b34be40ccd7f60ee5b218cff9bc8e7d00f4b84aaf55fb39"} {"question" "q-4631" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-4631" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-1"] ["math" "x=-1,\\,1"] ["math" "x=1"] ["math" "x=\\frac{1}{2}"] ["text" "No vertical asymptotes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-4631" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No vertical asymptotes"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-107032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x}{4x-12}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-107032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x}{4x-12} & =\\frac{3x(x-3)}{4(x-3)}=\\frac{3x}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/d1f0db6bba6e5bbd0079ae06a9f5199eaf8f24c4dfe0890ee691e2a5afcafc30.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-107032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bdf8a028228fc3190b34be40ccd7f60ee5b218cff9bc8e7d00f4b84aaf55fb39"} {"question" "q-107032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-107032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-3"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "x=3"] ["math" "x=\\frac{3}{4}"] ["text" "No vertical asymptotes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-107032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No vertical asymptotes"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-107074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}+1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-107074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{(2{x}^{2}+1)-({x}^{2}+3)}{({x}^{2}+3)(2{x}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{{x}^{2}-2}{({x}^{2}+3)(2{x}^{2}+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+3)(2{x}^{2}+1)=0.\n$$\n\nThis equation has no real solutions, therefore $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-107074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bdf8a028228fc3190b34be40ccd7f60ee5b218cff9bc8e7d00f4b84aaf55fb39"} {"question" "q-107074" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-107074" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "x=\\sqrt{2}"] ["text" "$x=-\\sqrt{2}$ and $x=\\sqrt{2}$"] ["text" "$x=\\sqrt{3}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}$"] ["text" "No vertical asymptotes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-107074" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No vertical asymptotes"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "From **left to right**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-9}-\\frac{1}{x+5}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-2}},\n$\\,$\n and $\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-9}-\\frac{1}{x+5} & =\\frac{x(x+5)-({x}^{2}-9)}{({x}^{2}-9)(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-{x}^{2}+9}{({x}^{2}-9)(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x+9}{({x}^{2}-9)(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x+9}{(x-3)(x+3)(x+5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n(x-3)(x+3)(x+5)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-5\\,,\\,x=-3\\,,\\,\\text{and}\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=-5$,  $x=-3,$ and $x=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bdf8a028228fc3190b34be40ccd7f60ee5b218cff9bc8e7d00f4b84aaf55fb39"} {"question" "q-339779" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339779" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339779" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339779" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339779" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339779" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339757" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{7}{4x+12}+\\frac{5}{x+3}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339757" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4x+12}+\\frac{5}{x+3} & =\\frac{7}{4(x+3)}+\\frac{5}{x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7+5(4)}{4(x+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4(x+3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n4(x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is at $x=-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339757" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bdf8a028228fc3190b34be40ccd7f60ee5b218cff9bc8e7d00f4b84aaf55fb39"} {"question" "q-339757" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339757" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{9}{4x-3}-\\frac{3}{8x-6}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4x-3}-\\frac{3}{8x-6} & =\\frac{9}{4x-3}-\\frac{3}{2(4x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9(2)-3}{2(4x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2(4x-3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n2(4x-3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is at $x=\\frac{3}{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bdf8a028228fc3190b34be40ccd7f60ee5b218cff9bc8e7d00f4b84aaf55fb39"} {"question" "q-339763" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-339763" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-68177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x-7}+\\frac{1}{x+5}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-68177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-7}+\\frac{1}{x+5} & =\\frac{x+5+x-7}{(x-7)(x+5)}=\\frac{2x-2}{(x-7)(x+5)}=\\frac{2(x-1)}{(x-7)(x+5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n(x-7)(x+5)=0\\,⟹\\,x=7\\,,\\,x=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=7$ and $x=-5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-68177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bdf8a028228fc3190b34be40ccd7f60ee5b218cff9bc8e7d00f4b84aaf55fb39"} {"question" "q-68177" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-68177" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=0"] ["math" "x=7"] ["text" "$x=-7$ and $x=5$"] ["text" "$x=7$ and $x=-5$"] ["text" "No vertical asymptotes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "q-68177" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=7$ and $x=-5$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/state.json" "file_sha256" "22231878877656c234211cb5e9b17c9aaaa530802a39f0af65910ab8fddb7605"} {"question" "e-84" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x^2-16}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/example-84.json" "file_sha256" "eae785bca68b5b059f83ea3f302590a4931704c985d06254b1c56ec9de1f6646"} {"question" "e-84" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-{4}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x+4)(x-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(x+4)(x-4) = 0\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=\\pm 4.\n$$\n\nConsequently, both $x=4$ and $x=-4$ are vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/example-84.json" "file_sha256" "eae785bca68b5b059f83ea3f302590a4931704c985d06254b1c56ec9de1f6646"} {"question" "e-85" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y = \\dfrac{x^2+x-6}{x^2-5x+6}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/example-85.json" "file_sha256" "47f9679b2dcbc996329e4258448b7d53d0b970cfdd7a86bdfb696b8c2f8ecc24"} {"question" "e-85" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-6}{{x}^{2}-5x+6} & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+3)}{(x-2)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+3)}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{x-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-3 = 0\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=3.\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/example-85.json" "file_sha256" "47f9679b2dcbc996329e4258448b7d53d0b970cfdd7a86bdfb696b8c2f8ecc24"} {"question" "e-86" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)=\\dfrac{x^2+x}{2x+2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/example-86.json" "file_sha256" "056dcddaffae32f4c584646163a65d1f4bea42f162e591e98810a40c682b82b9"} {"question" "e-86" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x}{2x+2} & =\\frac{x(x+1)}{2(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cancel{(x+1)}}{2\\cancel{(x+1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/eda5457edafe14fb51fcc75ca75677b199bfc3e0aef8940c66f8aa78cf8cd5c3.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/example-86.json" "file_sha256" "056dcddaffae32f4c584646163a65d1f4bea42f162e591e98810a40c682b82b9"} {"question" "e-87" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}+\\dfrac{1}{x+1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/example-87.json" "file_sha256" "a7097274ae6b82300c3912b17dddeac75f773beee395f0347c605ca4461db83b"} {"question" "e-87" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{x+1} & =\\frac{(x+1)}{x(x+1)}+\\frac{x}{x(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1+x}{x(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{x(x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\nx(x+1) = 0\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=0,-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=0$ and $x=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/example-87.json" "file_sha256" "a7097274ae6b82300c3912b17dddeac75f773beee395f0347c605ca4461db83b"}] "usage" {"e-84" [] "e-85" [] "q-4631" [] "e-87" [] "q-37066" [] "e-86" []} "basis" 857 "retractions" [[17592186120336 :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\frac{2x - 6}{x^{2} - 7x + 12}$."] [17592186120405 :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = ((x - 1)(x^{3} + 1))/(x^{2} - 1)$."] [17592186119619 :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x^2-16}$."] [17592186119619 :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{1}{x^{2} - 16} \\\\\n&= (1)/((x^{2} - 4^{2})) \\\\\n&= (1)/((x + 4)(x - 4))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(x+4)(x-4) = 0\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=\\pm 4\n$$\n\nConsequently, both $x=4$ and $x=-4$ are vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)$."] [17592186119620 :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y = \\dfrac{x^2+x-6}{x^2-5x+6}$."] [17592186119620 :question/worked-solution "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2} + x - 6}{x^{2} - 5x + 6} &= ((x - 2)(x + 3))/((x - 2)(x - 3)) \\\\\n&= ((x - 2)(x + 3))/((x - 2)(x - 3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x + 3}{x - 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-3 = 0\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=3\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=3$."] [17592186119621 :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)=\\dfrac{x^2+x}{2x+2}$."] [17592186119621 :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2} + x}{2x + 2} &= (x(x + 1))/(2(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= (x(x + 1))/(2(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=-1$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1815/Source/Images/b88f58b484a9741d1c21581309c5a6c4.png)"] [17592186119623 :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}+\\dfrac{1}{x+1}$."] [17592186119623 :question/worked-solution "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{1}{x + 1} &= ((x + 1))/(x(x + 1)) + (x)/(x(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= (x + 1 + x)/(x(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= (2x + 1)/(x(x + 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\nx(x+1) = 0\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=0,-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=0$ and $x=-1$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186118492 17592186118493 17592186118494 17592186118495 17592186118496 17592186118906 17592186118907 17592186118908 17592186118909 17592186118910] "immutable_fields" [[17592186118491 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118491 :answer-field/id] [17592186118491 :answer-field/key] [17592186118491 :answer-field/type] [17592186118905 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118905 :answer-field/id] [17592186118905 :answer-field/key] [17592186118905 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
